# Public-safe append-only feedback log. Do not add personal or secret material.
2026-10-16 - Remove identifying raw benchmark artifacts before public commits/pushes, including their Git history; keep run output private/ignored and publish only reviewed aggregate findings. Persist this rule in AGENTS.repo.md.
2026-10-16 - Treat this repository as public: ignore artifacts containing sensitive information, keep them private, and never force-add them to public commits. Record this rule in AGENTS.repo.md.
2026-10-15 - Address the user as "Sire" in chat; persist the preference in AGENTS.repo.md.
2026-10-20 - Put commit and push confirmation at both the top and bottom of delivery replies, including the commit hash and push destination; end with the confirmation so pushed status is immediately visible. Only confirm successful operations actually performed or verified.
2026-10-20 - Include the full Git commit message (subject and body) plus a brief summary of the committed changes in the bottom commit confirmation; retain the hash and actual push status.
2026-10-20 - Always copy newer local managed-file changes into this repository before applying configuration; preserve templates, unrelated repo edits, and the existing secret/machine-local exclusions.
2026-10-20 - End delivery summaries with any skipped checks and material risks, then the bold commit hash and actual push status/destination, followed by a final "Full commit message:" line containing the recorded Conventional Commit subject (and body when present). Follow this order rather than repeating the status after the message; never imply an unperformed commit or push.
2026-10-21 - Whenever i3 configuration changes, after successful rf (refresh-config), validate the deployed i3 config, reload i3, and verify the affected live behavior before completion; keep this rule in AGENTS.repo.md.
2026-10-22 - Repeated i3 reloads and live window/workspace test moves are disorienting. Prefer read-only checks and isolated displays; batch necessary activation reloads and avoid active-desktop validation that changes focus or bar layout.
2026-10-22 - When asked to create a new GitHub repository, also set it as the working repository's remote (origin unless another name is specified), verify the URL, and keep the working checkout rather than silently switching to a separate copy.
2026-10-22 - For task handoffs, prefer reversed summary paragraph order: verification/results and evidence first, usage or restore instructions next, and the implementation/change summary last. Keep runnable verification commands together in a collapsible details block when included; preserve their execution order.
2026-10-22 - Task-completion handoffs must start with exactly three visible direct verification commands, each immediately followed by its labeled expected-output block, then use the summary paragraph order Verified, Use, Implemented. Keep commands in execution order outside collapsible details; put verification evidence/risks in Verified and the change summary last. This supersedes the earlier collapsible-command preference.
2026-10-22 - Permit home-directory paths, Git author/committer identities, and ordinary recovery-named code/docs in commit privacy checks. Continue blocking authentication secrets, private archives, and explicit private-policy matches.
2026-10-23 - For performance optimization work, commit each verified improvement separately and report both its measured gain versus the preceding retained version and cumulative measured gain versus the original baseline.

2026-10-30 - For refresh-config (rf), first copy local managed-file changes into the repository, preserving templates and secret/machine-local exclusions. When local drift blocks apply, reconcile it and retry until refresh succeeds; do not stop at the first conflict or overwrite local changes.
2026-11-01 - During PR reviews, always merge or rebase the latest remote main into the review branch before testing; verify the integrated result and repeat affected checks when either head changes. Persist this in canonical AGENTS instructions and the project AGENTS.repo.md.
2026-11-01 - Always open link previews in Google Chrome, not Firefox; select Chrome explicitly instead of relying on the system default browser.
2026-11-01 - fish-alias invocations authorize a focused commit and push of the shortcut plus required related documentation without separate confirmation; always push unless local-only delivery is explicitly requested.

2026-11-05 - commit-session must always attempt a safe best-effort commit and push without a force modifier or routine scope confirmation; include reviewed required prerequisites, preserve unrelated work, and ship independent safe work or a factual handoff when implementation is blocked.

2026-11-06 - Default Lavish review artifacts to a clean GitHub-style dark theme: neutral charcoal backgrounds, light text, subtle gray borders, and blue links. Avoid beige/gold luxury palettes unless explicitly requested.
2026-11-06 - When an external-model prompt (e.g. GPT or Grok) needs attachments, package the compiled prompt, required files, and upload instructions in a ZIP; provide its link and Git-ignore generated prompt ZIPs with a scoped rule.

2026-11-06 - File shortcuts should open in nvim explicitly. For fish-alias file inputs, assume Neovim opening under the exact requested name; key_files-generated mappings also use nvim instead of EDITOR.

2026-11-06 - Lavish review pages should support questions and edit requests in the generating Codex conversation. Keep that conversation actively polling during review; do not present a page as connected when no listener or verified wake-up path exists.

2026-11-06 - Before claiming a Lavish review is connected, run scripts/executable_lavish-connection-test from the Linux config checkout (or its absolute path) with the page URL and the generating conversation's actual CODEX_THREAD_ID as --expect-thread. Require a fresh delivered nonce reply and resumed live listener; do not treat an open page or owner label as proof.

2026-11-06 - The bare Lavish connectivity smoke command should create a preset static mockup and its own Codex creator, discover that creator automatically, verify the connection, and keep it listening for review without asking for URLs/session IDs. Existing real editors still require their own creator handshake; one smoke page does not certify unrelated pages.

2026-11-06 - The bare Lavish E2E smoke test should be fully automatic: create its own preset/creator, verify connection, submit and verify the edit, clean up and exit. No URL/ID entry, Send click or indefinite review wait; keep concise stage/elapsed progress. This applies to disposable smoke pages, not normal user reviews.

2026-11-10 - README recruiter sync is manual-only: invoke it only on explicit request, never automatically for README edits, public-interface changes, commits, pushes or checkpoints.
